<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José de Iturrigaray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1742–1815</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José de Iturrigaray was a Spanish army officer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> from 1803 to 1808. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> forced the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> to abdicate in 1808, he appeared to favour criollo proposals for a provisional assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and peninsular merchants led by Gabriel de Yermo overthrew him in September 1808. The coup deepened the split between Spaniards and criollos that preceded the war of independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-de-iturrigaray.jpg" alt="José de Iturrigaray" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Viceroy_Jos%C3%A9_de_Iturrigaray.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
